<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xunzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 310 – after 238 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Xun Kuang, Master Xun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xunzi, born Xun Kuang in the state of Zhao, was a Confucian philosopher of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, often ranked as the third great Confucian thinker after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>. He studied and taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jixia-academy">Jixia Academy</a> in Qi, later served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-chunshen">Lord Chunshen</a>, and taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a>. In reply to Taoist and Mohist critics he revised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> into a cohesive system that shaped its flourishing under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Against Mencius he held that human nature is evil and must be corrected by education and ritual. His writings, compiled as the Xunzi, were set aside from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> onwards and reassessed in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xunzi-philosopher.jpg" alt="Xunzi" width="330" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Xun_Zi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the fourth century BCE the Tian rulers supported scholars at the Jixia Academy near the city's Ji Gate with stipends and houses; Mencius, <b>Xunzi</b>, Zou Yan, Shen Dao and Tian Pian were among those associated with it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Lu, King Cheng's brother the State of Jin, the Duke of Shao's family the State of Yan, and Jiang Ziya the State of Qi; the philosopher <b>Xunzi</b> later wrote that the Duke of Zhou set up 71 states, 53 of them held by members of the Ji clan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The classics include the Confucian Four Books and Five Classics and the Classic of Filial Piety, the Mozi, the Huainanzi and the <b>Xunzi</b>; in the Han dynasty Dong Zhongshu compiled the “Interactions Between Heaven and Mankind”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…taught that human nature is good and that a ruler who oppresses his people loses the mandate of Heaven and may rightly be overthrown; and <b>Xunzi</b>, in the 3rd century BCE, who held that human nature tends to evil and must be shaped by ritual, education and law.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Classical Chinese is that of the great texts of the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, from the 6th to the 3rd centuries BCE: the Analects of Confucius, the Mencius, the Zhuangzi, the <b>Xunzi</b>, the Han Feizi and the Zuo Commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
